Debug.Assert(OLD(this.Count) + 1 == this.Count);
</code>

This assert statement corresponds to the comment "@@@ Size of list increases by 1 in the function". It asserts that the size of the list increases by 1 after the function is executed. 

An addition to the original assert statement could be:

<code>
Debug.Assert(OLD(this.Capacity) >= this.Count);
</code>

This assert statement verifies that the capacity of the list never decreases.

Another assert statement could be:

<code>
Debug.Assert(this[0] == OLD(this[0]));
</code>

This assert statement checks whether the first element of the list remains the same even after the function is executed. This ensures that the list remains in its initial state.

Lastly, we can add an assert statement to check if the function properly handles null values:

<code>
Debug.Assert(this.Contains(null) == OLD(this.Contains(null)));
</code>

This assert statement does not necessarily increase the confidence in the correctness of the function, but it can help to identify potential issues in scenarios where the list contains null values.
